TASK:{"title":"To Ship or Not to Ship: Engineering Ethics Case Report","overview":"Write an individual report analysing the ethical decisions stakeholders face in Rachel’s quality-assurance scenario. Evaluate whether shipping servers with known defects is ethical, explain how to decide whether to ship, and recommend what Rachel should do. Support all arguments with reputable evidence, including external case studies, and consider the consequences for all affected stakeholders."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction — Identifies ethical issues and broad links to engineering design; discusses the context of ethical issues in engineering organisations; identifies relevant stakeholders when considering the ethical issues.","weight":20,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":10,"max":10,"desc":"Several ethical issues and their significance identified, described, in the context of engineering design, understanding of BOTH context in engineering organisations and stakeholder interest is demonstrated."},{"label":"Very good","min":8,"max":8,"desc":"Several ethical issues identified, described, and linked to engineering design, understanding of context in engineering organisations OR stakeholder interest is demonstrated."},{"label":"Good","min":6,"max":6,"desc":"Few ethical issues identified, described, and linked to engineering design, but understanding of context and stakeholders is limited."},{"label":"Satisfactory","min":4,"max":4,"desc":"Few ethical issues identified and described, but the context and stakeholders are not identified."},{"label":"None","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"No ethical issues identified."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Balanced Discussion — Discusses objective arguments for and against decisions on the ethical issues; draws a conclusion on how stakeholders should make decisions about the issues.","weight":20,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":10,"max":10,"desc":"Objective arguments are made with significant depth, leading to conclusions on decision making recommendations for several different stakeholders."},{"label":"Very good","min":8,"max":8,"desc":"Objective arguments are made with appropriate depth. Conclusions are made on decision making, though lack of consideration of different stakeholders."},{"label":"Good","min":6,"max":6,"desc":"Arguments are made, though they lack depth or are based on personal opinion. Conclusions are made on decision making, though lack of consideration of different stakeholders."},{"label":"Satisfactory","min":4,"max":4,"desc":"Ideas are presented, though they lack the depth required to build arguments. Conclusion is made with no apparent link to the arguments."},{"label":"None","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"No arguments presented. No conclusions drawn."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Compelling Argument that supports conclusion — The report forms a compelling argument that justifies the conclusion; the body follows a logical structure, with linked paragraphs and content ordered to support the argument; arguments demonstrate a systematic understanding of the ethical issues for all stakeholders.","weight":20,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":10,"max":10,"desc":"Arguments are compelling, supported by the report structure. The conclusion is well supported by the arguments. The arguments demonstrate a systematic understanding of the ethical issues for all stakeholders."},{"label":"Very good","min":8,"max":8,"desc":"Arguments are compelling, supported by the report structure. The arguments support the conclusion, demonstrating a sound understanding of ethical issues."},{"label":"Good","min":6,"max":6,"desc":"Arguments are logically structured, though are not compelling. Conclusion is not well supported by the arguments presented."},{"label":"Satisfactory","min":4,"max":4,"desc":"Arguments are difficult to follow. Link to conclusion is unclear."},{"label":"None","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Arguments are not evident. No conclusions drawn."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Research and Evidence — Arguments are substantiated with adequate references and qualitative/quantitative evidence; research demonstrates a balanced, in-depth understanding of the topic.","weight":20,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":10,"max":10,"desc":"Arguments are well substantiated with references as well as both qualitative and quantitative evidence. Evidence of research demonstrates a balanced and in-depth understanding of the topic and its ethical issues."},{"label":"Very good","min":8,"max":8,"desc":"Arguments are substantiated with adequate references and some qualitative/quantitative evidence. Research demonstrates a good understanding of the topic."},{"label":"Good","min":6,"max":6,"desc":"Arguments are substantiated with some relevant references and qualitative OR quantitative evidence. Research demonstrates a moderate understanding of the topic."},{"label":"Satisfactory","min":4,"max":4,"desc":"Limited references that lack relevance to arguments, showing limited evidence of research."},{"label":"None","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"No references, or other evidence of research to support arguments."}]},{"id":"C5","name":"Report Structure, Format and Language — The report includes appropriate sections serving their functions (abstract, introduction, body, and conclusion); maps, figures, tables, and visual elements are correctly used to support discussion; writing is grammatically correct, fluent, concise, easy to read and understand, and uses appropriate word choice; references are well-structured and appropriate, supporting the report’s motivations.","weight":20,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":10,"max":10,"desc":"The report is fluently written, has no incorrect uses of language, uses several types of visual elements competently, and uses relevant references extensively."},{"label":"Very good","min":8,"max":8,"desc":"The report is fluently written, has no incorrect uses of language, uses several types of visual elements competently, and uses relevant references."},{"label":"Good","min":6,"max":6,"desc":"The report is fluently written, has no incorrect uses of language, uses limited visual elements, and a few relevant references."},{"label":"Satisfactory","min":4,"max":4,"desc":"The report is fluently written, has few incorrect uses of language, very limited visual elements, and few, mostly irrelevant references."},{"label":"None","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"The report has incorrect uses of language, has no visual elements, expression is not fluent, and references are missing."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Arguments against shipping"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"How should Rachel decide when to ship?"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Arguments for shipping"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Arguments against shipping"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Arguments against shipping"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Arguments against shipping"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"RECOMMENDATIONS"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Arguments against shipping","issue":"WannaCry was a ransomware attack, not a product defect; the report uses it as the main evidence for consequences of server failure, which weakens the analogy despite the caveat."},{"where":"How should Rachel decide when to ship?","issue":"The decision rule in Figure 1 asks whether the worst credible consequence is 'tolerable' but gives no threshold or method for judging tolerability, which reduces its explicitness."},{"where":"The Most Ethical Path","issue":"Claims options B and C respect every stakeholder's interests better, yet C imposes cost on the company and B on margin; the claim is overstated."},{"where":"How should Rachel decide when to ship?","issue":"States that risk management standards 'require' the assessment, but ISO 31000 is a guideline document, not a requirement."},{"where":"INTRODUCTION","issue":"Applies the Engineers Australia Code to a case set in a US-style company without justifying its choice over other engineering codes."}],"overall":"A strong, well-structured report with clear arguments, a usable decision rule and a justified recommendation. Weaknesses are thin treatment of Rachel's employment interests and the chip supplier, limited ethical-theory literature, and some overstated claims, notably the WannaCry analogy and the 'every stakeholder' argument.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Excellent","mark":10.0,"scale":10.0,"points":20.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Three ethical issues are identified and linked to design process decisions. Organisational context is explicit (release calendar, conflict of interest). Table 1 maps six stakeholders with interests and control, so both context and stakeholder interests are demonstrated.","evidence":[{"where":"INTRODUCTION","quote":"Three ethical issues follow. First, test coverage is being set by the release calendar instead of by risk"},{"where":"INTRODUCTION","quote":"the contract brings revenue and job security to the same people who decide whether the product is good enough"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Excellent","mark":10.0,"scale":10.0,"points":20.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Arguments for and against are developed in depth, with counterarguments tested against the cases. Conclusions and recommendations are directed at several stakeholders: Rachel, management, the health department, the chip supplier and product engineers.","evidence":[{"where":"Arguments against shipping","quote":"The strongest objection is harm to people who have no say."},{"where":"The Most Ethical Path","quote":"Shipping on the current basis is therefore not an ethical way to conduct business."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Very good","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The structure is logical and the conclusion follows from the option analysis. Rachel's employment interests and the chip supplier's position are not analysed in the discussion, so the understanding is not systematic across all stakeholders.","evidence":[{"where":"Arguments for shipping","quote":"The competitor argument assumes the buyer cannot tell the products apart"}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Very good","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Eight references and quantitative evidence (NAO figures, Intel US$475m charge, VW 11 million vehicles) support the arguments. Ethical theory and engineering-ethics literature beyond the Code are absent, and the WannaCry case is not a product defect, which limits balance and depth.","evidence":[{"where":"Arguments against shipping","quote":"at least 81 of 236 hospital trusts in England were disrupted, an estimated 19,000 appointments were cancelled"}]},{"id":"C5","level":"Very good","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Sections are well organised and the prose is fluent. Tables and a decision flowchart support the discussion, though only two visual types are used. References are relevant, and the reference list is in consistent IEEE style.","evidence":[{"where":"Figure 1","quote":""},{"where":"Table 2","quote":"Table 2: Options for the health contract."}]}]}
